<% layout('/layouts/boiler-plate') %>

<body>
    <br>
    <div class="row offset-4">
        <h3>Login </h3>
        <br>
        <br>
        <form method="post" action="/login" class="needs-validation" novalidate>
            <div class="mb-3 col-6">
                <label for="username" class="form-label">username</label>
                <input type="text" placeholder="" name="username" class="form-control" required>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-3 col-6">
                <label for="password" class="form-label">password</label>
                <input type="password" placeholder="" name="password" class="form-control" required>
            </div>
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-md btn-dark add-btn">Login</button>
        </form>
    </div>
    <br>
    <br>
</body>

<!--
the action attribute tells the browser where to send the form data when the user submits it.
When req.body is used?
POST / PUT / PATCH requests
These methods send data in the request body (form data or JSON).
GET requests
By convention, GET requests do not have a body.
Browsers send data via the query string instead:

HTML forms typically send data in URL-encoded format (like username=alice&password=123).
express.urlencoded() is middleware that parses this format and populates req.body.
extended: true → uses the qs library, which can parse nested objects and arrays in form data.
Example: user[name]=alice&user[age]=20 
-->